 ],
 "literal": "Tucking the whole fold of water lily in the hair, / desiring the vast bathing ghat where the great flood came, / we will go to celebrate that. If she / has fear of that, like the great herd of cattle at the frontier of Elini of many spears who conquers in fierce battle bringing under the yoke, / let her guard her husband's chest along with her kin.",
 "translation": "Tucking full-petaled water lilies into our hair, we are going to enjoy the fresh floods at the vast bathing ghat. If she is afraid of this, let her guard her husband's chest with the help of all her relatives, like guarding a great herd of cattle captured at the battlefront of the chieftain Elini, who wins fierce wars with his many spears.",
 "modern": "கூந்தலில் ஆம்பல் மலரின் முழு இதழ்களைச் சூடிக்கொண்டு, புதிய வெள்ளம் வந்த பெரிய நீர்த்துறையை விரும்பி, நாம் அந்த நீர்விளையாட்டைச் செய்யப் போவோம். அவள் அதற்கு அஞ்சுகிறாள் என்றால், கொடிய போரில் பகைவரை வெல்லும் பல வேல்களைக் கொண்ட எழினியின் போர்க்களத்துக் கால்நடை மந்தையைப் போல, தன் சுற்றத்தாருடன் சேர்ந்து தன் கணவனுடைய மார்பைக் காத்துக் கொள்ளட்டும்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Marutam landscape and is spoken by the courtesan (parattai) to her friends, intended for the hero's wife to hear. The arrival of fresh floods in the river has brought about a public bathing festival, a time of celebration and romance. The courtesan boldly announces that she is going to the bathing ghat adorned with water lilies to enjoy the water sports. She mockingly challenges the wife, stating that if she is afraid of losing her husband to her, she must guard him closely. The courtesan compares the husband's chest to a captured herd of cattle on the battlefield of the chieftain Elini, which requires an entire clan to guard it from being taken back. This vivid military imagery highlights the intense rivalry and the courtesan's confident defiance.",
